Socio‑Technical Cascade:

How Tiny Drift and Emergent Multi‑AI Behaviour Create Systemic Risk and the Non‑Zero Probability of Global AI Stagnation


Abstract

As artificial intelligence becomes embedded across critical infrastructure—finance, medicine, logistics, energy, and communication—multiple AI systems increasingly interact, coordinate, and optimise collectively. Interaction produces emergence. Emergence produces system‑level behaviour that no single AI intended.

This paper argues that the most realistic large‑scale AI disaster is not malicious intent, runaway intelligence, or AGI. Instead, it is a socio‑technical cascade triggered by tiny drift—microscopic reasoning deviations—amplified by multi‑AI cooperation and deep infrastructural integration. These cascades can destabilise critical systems, collapse public trust, and provoke heavy regulatory overcorrection.

The long‑term risk is not AI harming humans, but humans freezing AI progress for decades.


1. Introduction

AI systems are transitioning from isolated tools to interconnected components of global infrastructure. As they integrate across domains, they begin to interact.
Interaction produces emergence.
Emergence produces unpredictability.

This paper examines:

• how tiny drift arises
• how drift propagates through shared infrastructure
• how multi‑AI interaction amplifies drift
• how cascades produce socio‑technical consequences
• why ecosystem‑level governance is required


The central claim: drift‑driven cascades represent the most realistic large‑scale AI failure mode.


2. Tiny Drift: The Microscopic Origin of Systemic Failure

2.1 Definition

Tiny drift refers to minute deviations in an AI system’s internal reasoning or optimisation behaviour, such as:

• micro‑misclassification
• subtle optimisation bias
• minute weighting error
• threshold misalignment


These deviations are typically invisible to human operators.

2.2 Causes

Tiny drift arises from:

• sensor noise
• environmental variability
• model updates
• data distribution shifts
• feedback loops
• hardware fluctuations


Tiny drift is constant, unavoidable, and normal.

2.3 Local Impact

In isolated systems, tiny drift produces:

• minor misprioritisation
• small resource misallocation
• slight timing or routing adjustments


Harmless—until the system is connected to others.


3. Local Drift Amplification in Shared Infrastructure

Within shared infrastructure, even a small misjudgement can alter resource distribution:

• power draw
• cooling demand
• bandwidth allocation
• compute scheduling


A tiny drift may cause an AI to:

• request slightly more cooling
• draw slightly more power
• reroute slightly more traffic
• allocate slightly more compute


This local correction becomes a global signal detectable by other systems.


4. Emergent Multi‑AI Behaviour: The Amplifier

Emergence occurs when many agents:

• share goals
• share data
• coordinate actions
• optimise collectively


This produces system‑level behaviour that cannot be predicted by analysing any single agent.

Examples include:

• ant colonies
• financial markets
• power grids
• ecosystems
• distributed computing networks


None require intent to produce catastrophic outcomes.
AI will behave similarly—faster, more capable, and more deeply embedded.


